ers:\n        members[com].sort()\n    # ensure all communities from refinement values are present; they are.\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": sorted_edges, \"members\": members}\n\nBut careful: The output schema says \"members\" has dynamicKeysFrom case.refinement.values(). That means keys must exactly match the set of values in refinement. Our members dict built from com_map values will have exactly those keys. Good.\n\nPotential issue: nodes might be repeated in refinement? No, each node appears once.\n\nReturn sorted edges. Ensure no extra keys. The schema has additionalProperties false, so fine.\n\nNow encode the source as JSON string. Use json.dumps to produce the code string properly escaped. But we must output the entire response as JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}.\n\nI'll write it manually with proper escaping, but better to use a program to ensure correctness. Since I'm an AI, I can generate the escaped string carefully.\n\nAlternatively, I can produce the implementation in a Python string with triple quotes and then escape for JSON. I'll do it manually.\n\nWrite code with indent 4 spaces. Use newline \\n. Escape backslashes and quotes.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    agg = {}\n    members = {}\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        cu = refinement[u]\n        cv = refinement[v]\n        if cu <= cv:\n            key = (cu, cv)\n        else:\n            key = (cv, cu)\n        agg[key] = agg.get(key, 0) + w\n    for node, com in refinement.items():\n        members.setdefault(com, []).append(node)\n    for com in members:\n        members[com].sort()\n    sorted_edges = [[a, b, w] for (a,b), w in sorted(agg.items())]\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": sorted_edges, \"members\": members}\n\nThat should work. But note: We used refinement.items() directly for members, which is same as com_map. And we didn't validate that all nodes in edges exist in