  members[com].sort()\\n    sorted_edges = [[a, b, w] for (a,b), w in sorted(agg.items())]\\n    return {\\\"aggregate_edges\\\": sorted_edges, \\\"members\\\": members}\"\n\nNow I need to put this inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So final response: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    edges = case[\\\"edges\\\"]\\n    refinement = case[\\\"refinement\\\"]\\n    agg = {}\\n    members = {}\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        cu = refinement[u]\\n        cv = refinement[v]\\n        if cu <= cv:\\n            key = (cu, cv)\\n        else:\\n            key = (cv, cu)\\n        agg[key] = agg.get(key, 0) + w\\n    for node, com in refinement.items():\\n        members.setdefault(com, []).append(node)\\n    for com in members:\\n        members[com].sort()\\n    sorted_edges = [[a, b, w] for (a,b), w in sorted(agg.items())]\\n    return {\\\"aggregate_edges\\\": sorted_edges, \\\"members\\\": members}\"}\n\nMake sure no trailing spaces. This should be valid JSON. The double quotes in the inner string are escaped with backslashes. Let me verify: each \" inside must be \\\". Yes, I have them.\n\nI'll output exactly that as a JSON object.\n\nBut wait: The task says \"Return sorted aggregate edges and sorted membership lists.\" I did sort members lists. For aggregate edges, I sorted by (com1, com2) using sorted(agg.items()) which sorts by tuple. That is lexicographic. Good.\n\nOne more thing: In the output schema, the inner array for aggregate_edges must have exactly 3 items: [string, string, number]. My sorted_edges list contains exactly that.\n\nAlso, ensure that members keys are exactly the community strings from refinement. They are.\n\nThus solution is ready.\n\nI'll now produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1597,"completion_tokens":2815,"total_tokens":4412,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2612},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":15